// Practice quiz: Reading (optional): Jenkins, registries, CD and Kubernetes
// Unreviewed first draft, 2026-11-04. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: containers-cicd/microservices-and-ci-cd/jenkins-registries-cd-and-kubernetes

::o1 mapping::Which pair is the closest match between a declarative Jenkinsfile and a GitHub Actions workflow? {
=A stage and a job#Correct. Both are named groups of steps.
~A stage and a repository#Not quite. A repository holds the code, not a part of a run.
~A step and a runner#Not quite. A runner is a machine.
~An agent and a secret#Not quite. An agent says where work runs.
}

::o1 order::What differs by default between listed Jenkins stages and GitHub jobs? {
~Stages cannot run shell commands#Not quite. They run steps such as sh.
=Stages run in order, while jobs run in parallel unless needs is set#Correct. This is the difference that trips people up.
~Jobs cannot run shell commands#Not quite. They use run steps.
~Neither has an order at all#Not quite. Each has a clear default.
}

::o2 parts::In registry.example.com/team/api\:3.1, which part is the registry? {
~team#Not quite. That is part of the repository path.
~api#Not quite. That is part of the repository path.
=registry.example.com#Correct. The host at the front names the registry.
~3.1#Not quite. That is the tag.
}

::o2 defaults::What does a plain reference such as redis assume? {
~The GitHub registry and tag 1#Not quite. The defaults are Docker Hub and latest.
~A local file#Not quite. A reference is not a file path.
~The newest image, guaranteed#Not quite. latest is only a name.
=Docker Hub and the tag latest#Correct. Both are defaults for short names.
}

::o3 deployment::What does a Kubernetes Deployment do when one Pod crashes? {
=Restores the desired number of replicas#Correct. It keeps the actual state matching the desired state.
~Waits for a new image#Not quite. No image change is needed.
~Deletes the Service#Not quite. The Service stays.
~Rewrites the registry#Not quite. A registry is separate.
}

::o3 cd::What separates continuous deployment from continuous delivery? {
~Continuous deployment has no tests#Not quite. Tests still gate it.
=Continuous deployment has no manual approval before production#Correct. That approval is the difference.
~Continuous delivery cannot use containers#Not quite. It can.
~They are the same thing#Not quite. They differ on the approval.
}
